latest_plan = "Based on the reflection and insights gained from analyzing all examples, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Color Groups:\n   a. Iterate through the input grid.\n   b. For each non-black cell, if not already part of a group, perform a flood fill (using DFS or BFS) to identify the entire color group.\n   c. For each group, store:\n      - Color\n      - List of coordinates\n      - Topmost row\n      - Leftmost column\n      - Rightmost column\n      - Bottom row\n\n2. Sort Color Groups:\n   a. Primary sort: by topmost row (ascending)\n   b. Secondary sort: by leftmost column (ascending)\n\n3. Initialize New Grid:\n   a. Create a new grid with the same number of rows as the input.\n   b. Set the number of columns to the number of color groups identified.\n   c. Fill the grid with black (0) cells.\n\n4. Place Color Groups:\n   a. Initialize a current_column variable to 0.\n   b. For each row in the input grid:\n      - For each color group starting in this row (based on sorted order):\n        * Place the group in the new grid starting at current_column.\n        * Expand the group vertically if it spans multiple rows.\n        * Increment current_column by the width of the group.\n   c. If a row in the new grid is empty (all black), fill it with the pattern from the row above.\n\n5. Adjust Horizontal Spacing:\n   a. Scan the new grid column by column.\n   b. If two adjacent columns can be merged (same color pattern vertically), merge them.\n   c. After merging, if the number of columns is less than the number of distinct horizontal positions of groups in the original input, add black columns to separate groups that were originally separated.\n\n6. Compress the Grid:\n   a. Remove any rows that contain only black cells.\n   b. Remove any columns that contain only black cells.\n\n7. Create Output:\n   a. Convert the compressed grid into a ColoredGrid object.\n   b. Return this object as the final output.\n\nImplementation Details:\n\n1. For the flood fill in step 1, use a recursive DFS or iterative BFS to identify connected cells of the same color.\n\n2. In step 4, when placing groups, maintain their original shape by using the relative coordinates within each group.\n\n3. For step 5, keep track of the original horizontal positions of groups. When adding black columns, distribute them evenly or based on the original spacing if possible.\n\n4. Throughout the process, maintain the invariant that the vertical order of color groups is preserved, and within each row, the horizontal order is maintained.\n\n5. When expanding groups vertically in step 4, ensure that the expansion doesn't overwrite any already placed groups in lower rows.\n\n6. In step 5, when considering merging columns, check the entire column to ensure merging won't create conflicts with other groups.\n\nThis plan should address all the patterns observed in the examples, including maintaining vertical alignment, preserving row structure, allowing for appropriate horizontal spacing, and handling column merging when possible. It balances the goals of compression and maintaining the relative structure of the input grid."
